    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d468">

                <pb facs="0447" n="331" sameAs="frus:frus1912/pg331"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 811.5124/21.]</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the
                            Minister of Denmark</hi>.</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 3.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Department of State</hi>, <lb/>
                            <hi rendition="#italic">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1912-11-20">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">November 20, 1912</hi></date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to your note of the 24th ultimo,
                    in further relation to the interpretation to be placed upon Article VII of the
                    Treaty of April 26, 1826, between the United States and Denmark, in connection
                    with the question of the rate of the inheritance tax in Iowa on the estate of
                    the late Ane Margrethe Andersen, I have now <pb facs="0448" n="332"
                        xml:id="pg332"/> the honor to advise you of the receipt of a letter of the
                    13th instant from the Department of Justice, wherein, with reference to the
                    inquiry made by you, it is stated that, while expressing no definite conclusion
                    in the matter, that Department is inclined to the opinion that an appeal to the
                    Supreme Court of the United States would be possible<anchor n="1"
                        xml:id="d468-f1"/> in the event that the Supreme Court of Iowa rendered a
                    decision adverse to the Danish heirs.</p>
                <p>Accept, Sir, the renewed assurance of my highest consideration.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">P. C. Knox.</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
